---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import EveryCut from '../../components/EveryCut.astro';
import GuideData from '../../components/GuideData.astro';
import {bySlug,kicker} from '../../dossiers/registry.js';
import {dateStamp} from '../../dossiers/display.js';
import {householdModel} from '../../dossiers/households.model.js';
import {vintageText} from '../../../scripts/lib/vintage.mjs';
const D=bySlug.households,model=householdModel();
const groups=[...new Set(model.plates.map(p=>p.group))];
const period=model.plates[0].period,flowPeriod=model.plates.find(p=>p.bundle==='households-aidis-19').period;
const payload={facts:model.facts,plates:model.plates};
---
<Base title="Household assets and borrowing · India Atlas" description={D.description} nav="guides" dateStamp={dateStamp(D)} crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Households / <b>Assets and borrowing</b>`} crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`} canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/households/" ogImage="/og/households.png">
<div class="page">
 <div class="titleblock break" data-inputs="households-aidis-6" data-prose="evergreen and data-bound facts">
  <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div><div class="hero-grid"><h1 set:html={D.headline}/><p class="lede">A house or a piece of land can be valuable without paying the next bill. The household debt and investment survey reads those assets alongside cash loans, lenders and the spending that adds to a family’s stock of capital.</p></div>
  <div class="meta"><span>Stock reference <b>{period}</b></span><span>Expenditure reference <b>{flowPeriod}</b></span><span>Instrument <b>NSS AIDIS, household interviews</b></span><span>Scope <b>Historical survey, rural and urban separate</b></span></div>
  <div class="numbers" data-long-values>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Mean assets per rural household</div><div class="v acc" id="n-rural_assets">–</div><div class="d">Physical and financial assets, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Mean assets per urban household</div><div class="v" id="n-urban_assets">–</div><div class="d">Physical and financial assets, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Rural households reporting cash loans</div><div class="v" id="n-rural_incidence">–</div><div class="d">Per thousand rural households, {period}</div></div>
   <div class="num"><div class="l">Urban households reporting cash loans</div><div class="v" id="n-urban_incidence">–</div><div class="d">Per thousand urban households, {period}</div></div>
  </div>
 </div>
 <section data-inputs="households-aidis-11, households-aidis-29" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>A balance sheet is different from a monthly budget</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Land, a home, animals and equipment can support a family’s livelihood. Selling them can also mean giving up that livelihood or a place to live. Deposits may be easier to spend, while shares have their own price risk. The survey values these different forms of ownership separately.</p><p>A loan can finance a home, farm inputs, a business, medical treatment or the repayment of another debt. The amount owed tells only part of that story. The lender, interest terms, purpose and repayment horizon matter too.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>These are survey estimates for a historical reference period. They are not today’s household balance sheets. The reported means include households without the relevant asset or debt; an indebted household’s mean debt would use a narrower population.</p><p>The survey does not show a family’s current repayment schedule or cash available after essential spending. A mean asset value cannot establish affordability, financial security or the wealth of a representative household.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <section data-inputs="all households-aidis bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>How to read this survey</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="wm-grid"><div><p><b>Keep stocks and flows apart.</b> Assets and outstanding cash loans refer to {period}. Capital expenditure and borrowing for that expenditure cover {flowPeriod}. The interviews took place later than the stock reference date; the date of collection does not make the asset values current.</p><p><b>Read the cell’s unit.</b> Rupees per household are means. Rupee lakh amounts in the aggregate tables are totals, even where an API heading calls them averages. Household reporting measures count reports per thousand households; loan-amount shares describe rupees per thousand rupees of outstanding debt. The interest-by-creditor table conditions on households with any outstanding loan. Those bases cannot be substituted for each other.</p><p><b>Classes belong to the survey’s own definitions.</b> Asset classes are deciles of the estimated all-India rural or urban asset distribution. A state’s class uses that national sector boundary. Expenditure quintiles use usual monthly consumption expenditure per person. They describe different rankings and are not interchangeable.</p><p><b>Households can report several loans.</b> Creditor, purpose, interest and tenure incidence can overlap. Amount shares can be added only across mutually exclusive components, excluding subtotals and the all-category total. No national estimate here is made by averaging state ratios.</p><p><b>Valuation and population matter.</b> The survey records household assets under its valuation rules. Gold and other valuables are separately identified. A household-head gender cut is not a record of the assets owned by all women or all men. Means within social groups cannot reveal inequality within those groups.</p></div><div class="wm-rail"><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">A historical ruler</span>Keep this round separate from later surveys and current market valuations.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Missing is missing</span>A source dash stays null. Published zeros remain rounded source estimates; they do not establish that nobody in the population owns or owes anything.</aside><aside class="margin-note"><span class="mk">Uncertainty accompanies detail</span>State and small-group estimates can rest on thin samples. The official report supplies relative standard errors for selected measures; the API does not attach them to each cell.</aside></div></div>
 </section>
 <section id="current-reading" data-inputs="households-aidis-6" data-prose="data-bound facts"><div class="sec-head"><h2>The snapshot reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">{period}</span></div>
  <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p>Mean cash debt across all rural households is <b id="n-rural_debt">–</b>. Across all urban households it is <b id="n-urban_debt">–</b>. These amounts include households without outstanding cash loans. Among indebted households, the separately published means are <b id="n-rural_indebted">–</b> in rural India and <b id="n-urban_indebted">–</b> in urban India.</p><p>The ownership plates separate whether a household reports an asset from its average value. The creditor and loan-purpose plates also separate the household reporting base from the amount of money owed.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p>The rural and urban populations have different livelihoods, asset valuations and borrowing patterns. The figures describe those survey populations; they do not establish a causal effect of living in one sector.</p><p>We do not know the median household’s net worth or present financial stress from these means. We also cannot read a time trend from a single survey snapshot.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 {groups.map(group=><section data-inputs={model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=>p.bundle).join(', ')} data-prose="evergreen explanations and data-bound values"><div class="sec-head"><h2>{group}</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  {model.plates.filter(p=>p.group===group).map(p=><div class="break"><figure id={'cb-'+p.id} data-inputs={p.bundle}>
   <div class="plate-head"><div class="t"><h3>{p.title}</h3><div class="sub">{p.sub}</div></div><div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id={'pv-'+p.id}>–</div><div class="c">{p.rows[0].name}, {p.period}</div></div></div>
   <div class="frame"><div class="plotzone"><div id={'plot-'+p.id}></div></div><div class="plotfoot"><span class="src">{p.source}, {p.period}. {p.unit==='inr_lakh'?'Aggregate amounts, ₹ lakh':p.unit==='inr'?'Rupees per household':'Per thousand, with the measure’s own household or rupee base'}.</span><span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href={p.sourceUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span></div></div>
   <div class="explain"><div class="ev"><span class="mk">How to read this</span><p>{p.explain}</p></div><div class="now"><span class="mk">As of {p.period}</span><p>This is the survey’s historical reference period. The chart uses published cells, with rural and urban estimates separate. The register preserves every archived state and household cross-tab; uncertainty must accompany small-group comparisons.</p></div></div>
  </figure></div>)}
 </section>)}
 <section data-inputs="all households-aidis bundles" data-prose="evergreen"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Common misreadings</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
 <div class="reading-cols"><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“Mean debt is the borrower’s bill.”</b> These household means include those without debt. Debt per indebted household is a different measure and is not silently inferred here.</p><p><b>“Asset value is cash available.”</b> A household may need its land or dwelling. Valuation is different from liquidity and from a sale price achieved in practice.</p><p><b>“Group means measure inequality.”</b> Different means leave the distributions within groups unknown. They do not identify the median or the concentration of ownership.</p></div><div class="reading-col"><p><b>“Creditor reporting rates add to the borrowing rate.”</b> The same household can owe a bank and a moneylender. Agency totals also overlap their named components.</p><p><b>“A household-head gender estimate measures women’s ownership.”</b> The head’s gender classifies the household. Individual ownership is a separate question.</p><p><b>“The survey round is the debt date.”</b> Collection, stock reference, expenditure period and data vintage are distinct. The stock date printed above is the relevant reference for outstanding loans.</p></div></div>
 </section>
 <EveryCut slug="households"/><GuideData slug="households"/>
 <section data-inputs="all households-aidis bundles" data-prose="methodology"><div class="sec-head"><h2>Sources, method and revisions</h2><span class="line"></span></div><p>The adapter retains every observed row of the official AIDIS API listing, with the full published joint dimensions. It does not construct combinations from separate filter lists. Source rows are archived before canonical files are written. Replaying the source cells and checking component totals are separate validation steps.</p><p>The official report contains additional tables and columns beyond the API inventory, including household sample bases, relative standard errors, individual financial access, asset shares and finer investment components. Negative share-asset means published for an Andhra Pradesh urban cut are retained for audit in the downloads and flagged in the source-quality register. They are not used in the analytical plates. Those report-only measures are listed as unfinished in the coverage packet; this guide’s every-cut claim applies to the archived API cubes. It should not be read as complete transcription of the report.</p><p><a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in/sites/default/files/publication_reports/Report_no588-AIDIS-77R-SeptFinal_0.pdf">MoSPI: AIDIS official report, definitions, detailed tables and uncertainty appendix</a>. <a href="/guides/debt/">Macro credit</a> uses a different statistical instrument and remains a separate guide.</p><p>Data vintage: <b id="prov-vintage">{vintageText(model.plates[0])}</b>. The API archive supplies no publisher revision timestamp. The report edition and archive capture dates are recorded separately; the capture date does not establish a data revision.</p></section>
</div>
<script is:inline type="application/json" id="households-data" set:html={JSON.stringify(payload).replace(/</g,'\u003c')}/>
<script>import '../../dossiers/households.client.js';import '../../dossiers/guide.client.js';</script>
</Base>
